# Build context for the two frontend images is the REPO ROOT (see the `web` and
# `frontend` services in docker-compose.yml), so this file — not app/.dockerignore
# or web/.dockerignore — is what Docker consults for them. Those two were deleted
# when the contexts widened; a .dockerignore is only ever read from the context
# root, so leaving them would have been dead files that still looked load-bearing.
#
# The backend still builds with `context: ./api` and is unaffected by this file.

# ── Never ship dependency trees or build output ──────────────────────────────
# Both images run their own `pnpm install`; a host node_modules is the wrong
# platform's binaries (esbuild and sharp ship native code) and is enormous.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
app/dist/
web/dist/
web/.astro/

# ── Not needed by a frontend build ───────────────────────────────────────────
# The Laravel app builds from its own context. api/vendor alone is several
# hundred MB, and rebuild.sh builds --no-cache, so this is paid on every run.
api/
docs/
.github/

# ── Local state, secrets and noise ───────────────────────────────────────────
.git/
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.log
.worktrees/
.claude/
.playwright-mcp/
app/test-results/
app/playwright-report/

# NOTE: pnpm-workspace.yaml, pnpm-lock.yaml and packages/ are deliberately NOT
# ignored — both Dockerfiles copy them to install the workspace. web/ used to
# ignore pnpm-workspace.yaml because it pinned pnpm@9, which rejected the file;
# it now pins pnpm@11 and requires it.
